<x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/">
  <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
    <rdf:Description xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" rdf:about="">
      <dc:title>
        <rdf:Alt>
          <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Nine Lives</rdf:li>
        </rdf:Alt>
      </dc:title>
      <dc:description>
        <rdf:Alt>
          <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">&lt;p&gt;What comes after the agony of your life falling apart in front of your very eyes? Is it a world of fire and sickness if youâ€™re deemed bad enough, like Shadow Weaver always told them? And if it was, would she be sent there? She tried to think back on her life in those few seconds she had left. Was there anything she had done to deserve an afterlife of torture? She hadnâ€™t killed anyone or cheated or anything like that. She hadnâ€™t asked for the cards sheâ€™d been dealt in life, so really it wasnâ€™t her fault for fighting her former best friend. So, no. Catra did not believe she belonged in Hell. But that didnâ€™t necessarily mean sheâ€™d end up in Heaven with streets of gold and flower petals all around her.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;     Taking a final glance down, Catra saw she was almost on the ground. Her time had come, she was about to die.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Or: in which Catra has nine lives and each time she dies she relives one of the memories she forced herself to forget. Based off Stevenson's post.&lt;/p&gt;</rdf:li>
        </rdf:Alt>
      </dc:description>
      <dc:creator>
